    "created_at": "2026-04-07T14:47:09Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-04-07T14:47:49Z",
    "body": "Thanks for the issue, to address both points:\n\n \n1. `interestfor` does not respect `commandfor`, nor `popovertargetaction`. This is the intended behavior. For more on this see https://open-ui.org/components/interest-invokers.explainer/#why-no-interestaction-attribute.\n2. There is no way to control the mechanism, because `interestfor` is an abstraction over the `hover`/`focus` events functionality. For more see https://open-ui.org/components/interest-invokers.explainer/#why-the-name-interest-why-not-hover-or-focus. If you want to control a popover when enacting a button with a `click` or tap, then `command`/`commandfor` is the preferred way to do this.\n\n> Is this the intended behavior of `interestfor`?\n\nI think the answer to this is yes.\n\n> Are there plans to support configurable triggers?\n\nAt the moment, no. There doesn't seem to be compelling use cases. If you have some please let us know!\n\n> How should developers properly handle dismissal when using `interestfor`?\n\nDismissal should be handled for you, via the `loseinterest` event, which can happen for a variety of reasons (e.g. the user focuses elsewhere, clicks to light dismiss, or presses the escape key).\n\nThe intent of the API is to be declarative and automatic. If you need different behaviour, you may need to use a manual popover, or use command/commandfor. However if you think the behaviour as designed is _broken_ that's definitely something we should discuss further.\n\n\n/cc @mfreed7",
